---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Traffic vs. Conversion for Trades — Fornax Blog">
  <article class="post">
    <p class="post-date">September 18, 2026</p>
    <h1>Traffic vs. Conversion: Why More Visitors Isn't the Goal</h1>

    <p>
      It's easy to treat visitor count like a scoreboard. More visitors feels like more success. For a trades
      business, though, a visitor who leaves without calling or filling out a form is worth nothing — and a site that
      gets fewer visitors but converts more of them into leads is the better business outcome every time.
    </p>

    <h2>What conversion actually means here</h2>
    <p>
      For a plumbing, HVAC, electrical or roofing site, conversion is almost always one of two actions: a phone call
      or a quote-request form. Everything on the page exists to make one of those two actions easy to take, fast to
      load, and hard to miss.
    </p>

    <h2>Why traffic-first thinking backfires</h2>
    <p>
      Chasing traffic often means adding content, widgets, and pages aimed at search engines rather than the person
      about to hire a contractor. That can bloat load time and bury the contact information a real visitor is looking
      for — the opposite of what converts.
    </p>

    <h2>What actually raises conversion</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Speed.</strong> A fast-loading site keeps mobile visitors from bouncing before they see the phone number.</li>
      <li><strong>Clarity.</strong> Stating your trade and service area in the first screen, not the third.</li>
      <li><strong>Proof.</strong> Real reviews, visible near the top, not buried in a footer.</li>
      <li><strong>Fewer steps.</strong> A short form beats a long one; click-to-call beats hunting for a phone number.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The metric that matters</h2>
    <p>
      Track leads per hundred visitors, not visitors alone. A site that converts 8% of a smaller, more targeted
      audience outperforms one that converts 1% of a much bigger one — and costs less to run.
    </p>
  </article>

  <section class="cta-footer">
    <h2>Want a site built to convert, not just to exist?</h2>
    <a href="/demo" class="button button-primary">Get my demo</a>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .post {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 60px 20px;
  }

  .post-date {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: #999;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.3px;
    margin: 0 0 12px;
  }

  .post h1 {
    font-size: 34px;
    line-height: 1.15;
    margin: 0 0 32px;
  }

  .post h2 {
    font-size: 22px;
    margin: 36px 0 16px;
  }

  .post p {
    color: #444;
    line-height: 1.75;
    margin: 0 0 18px;
  }

  .post ul {
    color: #444;
    line-height: 1.8;
    padding-left: 20px;
    margin: 0 0 18px;
  }

  .cta-footer {
    text-align: center;
    background: #f9f9f9;
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 48px 20px;
    max-width: 1200px;
    margin: 0 auto 60px;
  }

  .cta-footer h2 {
    font-size: 26px;
    margin: 0 0 20px;
  }

  .button {
    padding: 14px 32px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: none;
    border: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: 16px;
    display: inline-block;
  }

  .button-primary {
    background: #dc2626;
    color: white;
  }

  .button-primary:hover {
    background: #b91c1c;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
</style>
